Void: make the native (host-side) sub-build safe to run inside a
distribution build environment.

- Upstream relies on a hermetic environment when it spawns the "native"
  sub-build for host-side codegens (protoc, flatc, grpc_cpp_plugin).
  In a cross package build (Void Linux, masterdir = musl) that
  sub-build is executed with the cross environment's compilers and
  flags exported by the build style, which mis-targets the host tools
  (e.g. -march=armv8-a, -I/usr/aarch64-linux-musl/usr/include).
  Additionally the sub-build's CMake cache survives main reconfigures,
  so a stale cache silently imports those flags between builds.
- Without USE_MUSL the sub-build defaults GLIBC_COMPATIBILITY on (only
  auto-off when USE_MUSL) and pulls glibc-compatibility + gnu-style
  libc into the link, producing binaries that cannot run in the musl
  masterdir; the vendored libc++ likewise cannot detect musl headers
  (unknown rune table). Loading the vendored-musl toolchain file gives
  the sub-build the same shape as the main build: static-pie against
  the vendored musl.

Changes:

1. Never spawn the native sub-build from inside itself
   (CMAKE_BINARY_DIR matching /native), avoiding infinite recursion.
2. Always configure the sub-build from scratch (REMOVE_RECURSE of the
   native build directory), so no stale CMakeCache leaks cross flags.
3. Force the masterdir compilers (the tree refuses GCC), reset the
   CMAKE_C_FLAGS/CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS/CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS caches so they
   are neither reused from the cache nor re-seeded from the
   environment's cross CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS/LDFLAGS, load
   cmake/linux/toolchain-x86_64-musl.cmake (USE_MUSL, vendored
   toolchain) and pass X86_ARCH_LEVEL=1 for restricted /proc/cpuinfo
   chroots.

diff --git a/CMakeLists.txt b/CMakeLists.txt
index e17164059..824ae1bba 100644
--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -969,6 +969,7 @@ endif()
 # Build native targets if necessary
 get_property(NATIVE_BUILD_TARGETS GLOBAL PROPERTY NATIVE_BUILD_TARGETS)
 if (NATIVE_BUILD_TARGETS
+    AND NOT CMAKE_BINARY_DIR MATCHES "/native"
     AND NOT(
         CMAKE_HOST_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME
         AND CMAKE_HOST_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR STREQUAL CMAKE_SYSTEM_PROCESSOR
@@ -977,6 +978,9 @@ if (NATIVE_BUILD_TARGETS
 )
     message (STATUS "Building native targets: ${NATIVE_BUILD_TARGETS}")
 
+    # Void: always configure the native sub-build from scratch; a stale
+    # CMakeCache would leak the cross toolchain's flags into it.
+    file (REMOVE_RECURSE "${PROJECT_BINARY_DIR}/native")
     set (NATIVE_BUILD_DIR "${PROJECT_BINARY_DIR}/native")
 
     execute_process(
@@ -1000,6 +1004,27 @@ if (NATIVE_BUILD_TARGETS
             "-DENABLE_RUST=OFF"
             "-DENABLE_CLICKHOUSE_SELF_EXTRACTING=${ENABLE_CLICKHOUSE_SELF_EXTRACTING}"
             "-DUSE_BINARY_HASH=${USE_BINARY_HASH}"
+            # Void: the native (host-side) sub-build must run inside the musl
+            # masterdir, so it cannot inherit the cross environment's
+            # compilers or flags. Force the masterdir compilers (upstream
+            # code requires Clang) with the vendored-musl toolchain (USE_MUSL
+            # builds every target as a static-pie binary against the vendored
+            # musl: without it the default GLIBC_COMPATIBILITY pulls glibc
+            # symbols into the link and the vendored libc++ cannot detect
+            # musl headers, so the tools would not run in the musl
+            # masterdir). The flag resets stop CMake from seeding the caches
+            # from the cross environment's CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS/LDFLAGS (e.g.
+            # -march=armv8-a, -I/usr/aarch64-linux-musl/usr/include).
+            "-DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang"
+            "-DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++"
+            "-DCMAKE_ASM_COMPILER=clang"
+            "-DCMAKE_C_FLAGS="
+            "-DCMAKE_CXX_FLAGS="
+            "-DCMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS="
+            "-DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}/cmake/linux/toolchain-x86_64-musl.cmake"
+            # Void chroots expose a restricted /proc/cpuinfo; do not assume
+            # avx2/sse4.2 for the host-side tools.
+            "-DX86_ARCH_LEVEL=1"
         ${PROJECT_SOURCE_DIR}
         WORKING_DIRECTORY "${NATIVE_BUILD_DIR}"
         COMMAND_ECHO STDOUT
